                    brief1: '<h3>Round 1 — The Diagnosis: Understanding Why the Pipeline Leaks</h3><p>You are <b>Valentina Rodriguez</b>, the new Chief People Officer of <b>Meridian Financial Group</b>, a 4,000-employee LATAM financial services company. Your pipeline audit reveals critical findings:</p><div class="wlp-data-highlight"><b>Promotion Gap:</b> Women promoted at 12.3% vs. men at 17.8% — despite women averaging higher performance ratings (3.31 vs. 3.19).<br><b>Hiring Gap:</b> Salary offers to women are 8.3% lower than to men in the same role.<br><b>Culture Gap:</b> Psychological safety scores are 12 points lower for women.<br><b>Network Gap:</b> Women are 2.3 hops further from senior leadership nodes.</div><p>You have <b>$1,500K</b> to allocate across six interventions. Select which to fund. You cannot fund all of them — prioritize based on root cause analysis.</p>',
                    brief2: '<h3>Round 2 — Pay Equity: When the Numbers Tell an Uncomfortable Story</h3><p>Month 8. A comprehensive pay equity analysis reveals:</p><div class="wlp-data-highlight"><b>Raw Gap:</b> Women earn 31% less than men across all markets.<br><b>Adjusted Gap (same role/level/tenure):</b> Colombia 9.2%, Peru 11.7%, Chile 7.4%.<br><b>Structural Gap:</b> Occupational segregation accounts for ~20 points — women concentrated in lower-paying functions.<br><b>Cohort Analysis:</b> After 8 years in matched entry roles, the compensation gap reaches 47%.</div><p>The CFO wants phased remediation. The General Counsel warns of legal exposure. A union in Colombia may leak the report. Make three decisions on scope, disclosure, and manager communication.</p>',
                    brief3: '<h3>Round 3 — Sponsorship and Succession: Building the Next Generation</h3><p>Month 14. The Director of Commercial Banking Colombia retires in 7 months. Three candidates:</p><div class="wlp-data-highlight"><b>Fernando Castillo (M, 41):</b> 12 years at Meridian. Strong client relationships ($145M revenue). Exceeds expectations 4/5 years. No digital or international experience.<br><b>Claudia Mendoza (F, 38):</b> 10 years total. Led digital onboarding (+23% SME acquisition). Exceeds expectations 5/5 years. International experience. No senior sponsor.<br><b>Andres Villareal (M, 44):</b> 16 years. Meets expectations consistently. One below rating. Strong sponsor network from retiring executives.</div><p>Additionally, only 9 of 34 senior mentors have actively sponsored a woman in 24 months. Design the succession recommendation, sponsorship program, and succession process.</p>',
                    brief4: '<h3>Round 4 — Flexible Work and the Culture of Presence</h3><p>Month 20. New regulatory requirements demand flexible work utilization data disaggregated by gender. Your internal data reveals:</p><div class="wlp-data-highlight"><b>Telework:</b> 31% of women use it vs. 23% of men.<br><b>Paternity leave:</b> Men take only 4.2 of 10 entitled days.<br><b>Reduced hours:</b> Zero employees at Director+ have used it in 5 years.<br><b>Promotion penalty:</b> Flexible work users are 28% less likely to be promoted (33% for women, 19% for men).<br><b>Complaints:</b> 12 complaints about managers linking flexibility to career commitment. 2 are legally actionable.</div><p>Redesign parental leave, flexibility culture, complaint response, and the promotion-flexibility link.</p>',
                    brief5: '<h3>Round 5 — Measurement and Resistance: Sustaining the Change</h3><p>Month 28. Progress is real but uneven, and resistance is building:</p><div class="wlp-data-highlight"><b>Progress:</b> Women at Manager: 29% to 34%. Senior Manager: 21% to 24%. Pay gap: 9.2% to 3.1%. Attrition gap: 7pp to 1pp.<br><b>Resistance:</b> 8 senior male commercial bankers sent an anonymous letter claiming "standards are being compromised by forced diversity quotas" and "qualified male candidates are being passed over."<br><b>Competition:</b> Two competitors outperform Meridian on gender equity and are attracting better talent.</div><p>Respond to the resistance, design the permanent measurement framework, and address the competitive threat.</p>',
                    brief6: '<h3>Round 6 — Synthesis: Presenting the Business Case to the Board</h3><p>Month 36. Present your 3-year results to a mixed board: family members (financial focus), an institutional investor (ESG focus), a retired judge (legal focus), and the CEO.</p><div class="wlp-data-highlight"><b>3-Year Results:</b> Women at Manager: 29% to 34%. Adjusted pay gap: 9.2% to 3.1%. High-performer attrition gap: 7pp to 1pp. Culture score: 54 to 65.<br><b>Revenue correlation:</b> The 3 most equity-advanced units outperformed targets by 8.3%. The 3 least advanced underperformed by 4.1%.<br><b>Employee NPS (Women):</b> 28 to 41.</div><p>Frame the business case and recommend the next-phase strategy for Years 3-5.</p>'
                },
